The United Kingdom elects the House of Commons by first-past-the-post in
single-member constituencies; results are reported by Westminster
constituency.  Constituencies carry
their ONS code (\texttt{UK\_E14000530}).  British seats of the 1997 set, which
predate those codes, keep their number in the ONS boundary file with a
\texttt{P} prefix (\texttt{UK\_P024}, Bedford); England and Wales kept these
seats and numbers in 2005, Scotland's new seats take their later S14 codes.  Four boundary sets are
used: 1997 (2001 election), 2005 (Scotland redrawn to 59 seats), 2010 (2010--2019)
and 2024.

Boundaries come from the ONS Open Geography Portal (Open Government Licence).
The 2001 and 2005 ONS files cover Great Britain only; Northern Ireland's 18
seats are drawn from the 2010 set for those elections.  Its boundaries changed
slightly in 2008, so these two maps are an approximation for Northern Ireland.

The NUTS hierarchy is that of the last NUTS vintage including the UK (2021).
Constituencies do not nest in NUTS~3 regions: each is assigned to the NUTS~3
region holding the largest share of its area.  The median seat lies at
least 99\% in that region, and the NUTS~1 regions are exact up to one seat per
boundary set.
